Atrium ERP & CRM — Production Supabase Database & CRUD Architecture Plan
A complete, production-ready PostgreSQL & Supabase architecture blueprint for all 16 business verticals, reconciled against your live Supabase schema snapshot (39 tables, 14 functions, 13 triggers), fixing connection pooling & sync bottlenecks, closing critical live RLS security gaps, and guaranteeing end-to-end CRUD reliability for every screen, modal, and workflow.
What This Plan Accomplishes (Executive Summary)
Makes the Database Truly Production-Ready: Closes live public/anonymous RLS exposures (USING (true) and broken edu_* policies), removes hardcoded admin secrets from SQL functions, hashes staff PINs, enforces 7-point traceability + soft deletes (deleted_at) + immutable_audit_logs, and eliminates duplicate triggers and legacy text foreign keys.
Gives All 16 Business Categories Real Supabase Tables & Isolated Data: Splits shared cross-category tables (products, sales, members, appointments) into dedicated vertical schemas while preserving live rows, and provisions full Supabase schemas + typed CRUD services for the 9 missing categories (trading_commercial, medical_clinic, auto_garage, real_estate, manufacturing_production, legal_accounting, logistics_delivery, construction_contractor, agriculture_farming) that currently rely only on localStorage.
Fixes the Pooling & Sync Storm Problem (Server + Client): Eliminates both Supabase Supavisor transaction-pool bottlenecks (via InitPlan-cached RLS policies, composite partial indexes, and transaction-safe stateless RPCs) and client-side connection storms (fixing duplicate onAuthStateChange listeners across useAtriumStore() consumers and replacing the 18-query Promise.all blast with single-flight, category-scoped, bounded-concurrency delta sync).
Guarantees Working CRUD Across Every Feature: Audits every screen and modal across all 16 verticals and ensures every Create, Read, Update, Soft-Delete, Batch Action, and multi-table transaction (POS checkout, stock vouchers, roll-call payroll, escrow, DGP billing, weighbridge) works reliably online and via offline retry queue without silent failures or hardcoded fallback UUIDs.
Confirmed Architectural Decisions
Decision 1 — Hybrid Identity + 16 Per-Category Staff Tables: public.staff handles core auth (auth_user_id, email, hashed pin_hash, core role, max_staff quota), paired with 16 dedicated *_staff roster/credential tables and 4 Workforce ERP tables (staff_attendance_logs, staff_salary_advances, staff_payroll_runs, staff_payroll_slips).
Decision 2 — Dedicated Tables Per Category: No shared products, sales, members, or appointments tables across categories; duplicate live tables (members vs. members_directory, edu_* vs. education_*) are cleanly unified.
Decision 3 — 7-Point Traceability, Soft Deletes & Immutable Audit Trail: Every parent, child line-item, and Workforce table carries (account_id, business_id, branch_id, staff_id, updated_by_staff_id, device_id, created_at, updated_at, deleted_at), enforced by trigger and recorded in immutable_audit_logs.
Decision 4 — Strict Tenant-Scoped RLS & Clean Naming: Every USING (true) policy and legacy tenant_id / facility_id alias is removed after migrating live rows.
1. Solving the Pooling, RLS Latency & Sync Storm Problem
Codebase and live snapshot inspection uncovered four compounding causes behind connection pooling exhaustion and sync contention:
Layer	Root Cause Found Live / in Code	Production Fix in This Plan
1. Duplicate Auth & Sync Listeners (store.ts)	useAtriumStore() registers an authService.onAuthStateChange effect inside the hook itself. Because 30+ screens and modals call useAtriumStore(), auth state updates trigger N concurrent copies of syncWithSupabaseAction() simultaneously.	Move onAuthStateChange, initial sync, and realtime channel lifecycle to a single module-level bootstrapper initialized once at app boot, with a single-flight lock (inFlightSyncPromise) deduplicating overlapping sync calls.
2. Eager Multi-Category Promise.all Blast	syncWithSupabaseAction() fires 18 simultaneous queries across unrelated verticals (freelance_*, rental_*, appointments, members, products, sales) on every sync. Extending that to 16 categories (~115+ tables) would burst 100+ parallel PostgREST requests per client and exhaust Supabase Supavisor connection pools.	Implement a Category-Scoped Sync Coordinator: load Core (accounts, businesses, branches, staff, branch_modules) + only the active business category's tables through a bounded concurrency pool (max 4 in-flight requests), using incremental updated_at > last_synced_at delta queries after initial load.
3. Per-Row RLS Overhead Under Supavisor (6543)	Live RLS policies invoke get_auth_account_id() and EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM staff ...) uncached per row and lack composite indexes on (account_id, business_id, branch_id). Under Supavisor Transaction Mode (port 6543), slow RLS scans hold pooled backend connections open.	Wrap auth lookups in scalar subqueries (SELECT public.get_auth_account_id()) (InitPlan evaluation once per statement), index every table with (account_id, business_id, branch_id) WHERE deleted_at IS NULL, enforce stateless transaction-safe RPCs (no session-level SET or advisory locks, prepare: false for any server/Edge pooler URI on port 6543).
4. Realtime Channel Churn & Missing Handlers	realtimeService.ts generates a random Date.now() topic on branch switches, watches only 4 stale tables (members_directory, registration_requests, rental_bookings, rentable_units), ignores rental callbacks in store.ts, and re-fetches full tables on every event.	Maintain a single debounced channel per active (branchId, category) that coalesces bursts (250ms window) into targeted delta pulls by updated_at.
